1:-- Phase 5 T28 / AC-019, AC-024, AC-030, AC-034, BR-009, BR-010, AC-035
2:-- (spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience, owner decision D9):
3:-- membership integrity — a public.conversation_members row is created or
4:-- restored only by a server path that authorizes that user for that
5:-- conversation, and the authorization still holds when the row is written.
6:--
7:-- Before this migration (D9): the baseline policies on conversation_members
8:-- let any authenticated user INSERT a row for itself into ANY conversation and
9:-- UPDATE its row into another conversation (no WITH CHECK), and members could
10:-- UPDATE conversations.participants / room_id / type, whose AFTER trigger
11:-- (sync_conversation_members) inserted a membership for EVERY id in
12:-- participants — also re-creating memberships that had been removed.
13:--
14:-- Membership writers after this migration (the inventory is in TRACK T28):
15:--   * Creation of a membership row: only SECURITY DEFINER code owned by
16:--     postgres — sync_conversation_members (conversation creation; additions
17:--     to participants), public.join_room_conversation and
18:--     public.restore_conversation_membership — or a database administrator
19:--     role. The BEFORE INSERT trigger conversation_members_guard_insert lets
20:--     the API roles (anon, authenticated, service_role) insert only a row that
21:--     ALREADY exists (the INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE "upsert" of a
22:--     preference that old builds send), locking that row FOR KEY SHARE so a
23:--     concurrent removal either commits first (the upsert is refused with
24:--     NOT_PARTICIPANT, 42501, and no row is created) or waits for it.
25:--   * conversation_members: anon loses every write privilege; authenticated
26:--     keeps INSERT (that upsert) and UPDATE of its own rows (policies now
27:--     require user_id = the caller's users.id and, for INSERT, an existing
28:--     membership); DELETE and TRUNCATE are revoked from anon and authenticated
29:--     and the own-row DELETE policy is dropped: a client DELETE of a
30:--     membership — its own or anyone else's — is REFUSED (decision recorded in
31:--     TRACK T28; there is no client "leave"). The identity of a membership
32:--     (id, conversation_id, user_id) is immutable for every role
33:--     (conversation_members_identity_immutable), so no role can move a row to
34:--     another conversation or user.
35:--   * Removal = revocation: deleting a membership row (service role / admin;
36:--     not an FK cascade) records a revocation in
37:--     private.conversation_member_revocations. A revoked user is never
38:--     restored by an unrelated write (another user's join, a participants
39:--     change, a preference/archive/read write) nor by its own join; only
40:--     public.restore_conversation_membership (service_role: the explicitly
41:--     authorized rejoin) lifts it, re-checking the room's access rules under
42:--     lock. Existing participants without a membership row (removed before
43:--     this migration) are backfilled as revoked.
44:--   * public.conversations (client): anon loses INSERT/UPDATE; authenticated
45:--     keeps INSERT (the resolver creates conversations with the user-scoped
46:--     client in the current, Phase 4 and hosted builds), but the BEFORE INSERT
47:--     trigger conversations_guard_client_insert authorizes it under row locks:
48:--     direct = exactly the caller and one other existing user, not in two
49:--     different companies (the resolver's company rule), no room, visibility
50:--     'direct'; room = participants exactly [caller], the caller passes the
51:--     room's access rules (ConversationResolverService.assertRoomAccess,
52:--     mirrored by private.room_access_denial) and visibility is the one the
53:--     resolver derives; any other type is refused for clients. Client UPDATE
54:--     is limited to the columns name, last_activity and is_archived (column
55:--     privileges): no client can change participants, room_id, type,
56:--     visibility or the fingerprint. DELETE and TRUNCATE are revoked from
57:--     anon and authenticated (no DELETE policy existed; no build deletes
58:--     conversations with a user-scoped client).
59:--   * sync_conversation_members: on INSERT it still creates the memberships
60:--     of the new conversation's participants; on UPDATE OF participants it
61:--     acts only on ids NEWLY added (removed members already in the array are
62:--     never re-created) and only for room conversations, after the room's
63:--     access rules pass under lock and no revocation exists; otherwise the
64:--     whole UPDATE fails (MEMBERSHIP_GRANT_FORBIDDEN / MEMBERSHIP_REVOKED /
65:--     ROOM_ACCESS_DENIED, 42501). This also gates the old builds' service-role
66:--     addParticipant (read-modify-write of participants).
67:--   * public.join_room_conversation(conversation, user) (service_role): the
68:--     current build's room join/rejoin (POST /api/conversations/join and the
69:--     room resolver). Idempotent for a current member; refuses non-room
70:--     conversations, revoked users and users the room's rules deny.
71:--   * public.mark_messages_read now locks the caller's membership row
72:--     FOR KEY SHARE (was an unlocked EXISTS), so per-message receipts are
73:--     never recorded for a membership removed concurrently.
74:--
75:-- Authorization at write time (PLAN review r5 #1): every grant path locks, in
76:-- one order — the conversation row (when it exists) FOR NO KEY UPDATE, then
77:-- the users rows FOR SHARE (sorted by id), then the space row FOR SHARE —
78:-- and evaluates the access rules on the locked rows. Revocations are UPDATEs
79:-- of those rows (company/role on users; company/status/access_control on
80:-- spaces) or a DELETE of the membership row: a revocation that committed
81:-- first is seen; one in progress makes the grant wait and then see it. The
82:-- users -> spaces order is the Presence lock order (transition_user_location,
83:-- company RPCs), and Presence never locks conversations, so no lock cycle is
84:-- introduced; a grant may wait briefly behind a Presence transition of the
85:-- same user/room (and vice versa).
86:--
87:-- Trust boundary: service_role and admin roles creating CONVERSATIONS
88:-- (test seeding) are not restricted here — that is server code. Membership
89:-- rows themselves cannot be created by any API role directly (see above).
90:--
91:-- Security: functions are search_path '' with schema-qualified references;
92:-- EXECUTE revoked from public, anon and authenticated and granted explicitly
93:-- (Supabase default privileges grant it on new functions). private schema is
94:-- not exposed through the Data API.
95:--
96:-- Backward compatible (SPEC section 10): the current, Phase 4 and hosted
97:-- (pre-Phase-4) builds keep creating direct and room conversations
98:-- (user-scoped INSERT), joining rooms (service-role participants update or
99:-- the new RPC), and changing preferences/archive/read state, including the
100:-- hosted build's user-scoped preference/archive UPSERT of an existing
101:-- membership and the Phase 4 build's service-role UPSERT. What changes for old
102:-- builds: an UPSERT for a user without a membership row now fails instead of
103:-- creating one; a service-role addition to a direct/group conversation, or of
104:-- a revoked or room-denied user, fails.
105:--
106:-- Locking: ALTER/CREATE TRIGGER and the policy changes take SHARE ROW
107:-- EXCLUSIVE / ACCESS EXCLUSIVE on public.conversations and
108:-- public.conversation_members until COMMIT (short: the backfill reads
109:-- conversations once). lock_timeout bounds each wait (re-run on timeout).
110:-- Transactional and re-runnable.
111:--
112:-- Rollback (order, SPEC BR-014): FIRST roll the application back to the T4
113:-- build (it does not call join_room_conversation; the T4 build's join route
114:-- and resolver use addParticipant, and its preference/archive writes are
115:-- UPSERTs), THEN run this SQL as-is. While no Phase 5 build is deployed
116:-- against the target the app step is a no-op. WARNING: undoing this migration
117:-- RESTORES the D9 self-enrolment vulnerability (clients can again enrol
118:-- themselves, move memberships, and add others through participants) and
119:-- forgets every recorded revocation. Undo it only together with the T28 app.
